'''Richard A. Bennett''' (born May 24, 1963) is an American businessman and politician from the state of [[Maine]]. Initially elected as a [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]], he currently serves in the [[Maine Senate]] as an [[Independent politician|independent]]. He is currently running for [[Governor of Maine]] in the [[2026 Maine gubernatorial election|2026 election]]. Bennett previously served as [[President of the Maine Senate]] from 2001–2002.

Bennett was the Republican nominee for [[Maine's 2nd congressional district]] in [[1994 United States House of Representatives elections|1994]], losing to [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]] [[John Baldacci]], and was a candidate in the [[2012 United States Senate election in Maine|2012]] U.S. Senate election, losing the Republican primary to [[Charlie Summers]]. He has served in the Maine Senate since 2020.

== Political career ==
As a resident of [[Norway, Maine]],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://legisweb1.mainelegislature.org/wp/senate/educate-and-engage/past-presidents-of-the-senate/richard-a-bennett/|title=Richard A. Bennett at Maine State Senate|publisher=}}</ref> Bennett was first elected to the [[Maine House of Representatives]] in 1990. He was re-elected unopposed in 1992. In [[1994 United States House of Representatives elections#Maine|1994]], Bennett was the Republican nominee for Congress in [[Maine's 2nd congressional district|Maine's second district]], losing to [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]] [[John Baldacci]] in a close race.<ref>{{Cite web |title=OurCampaigns |url=https://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=28842 |access-date=2026-03-03 |website=www.ourcampaigns.com}}</ref> The seat, which had been held by Republican [[Olympia Snowe]], was one of only two Republican-held seats won by Democrats in the 1994 House elections in what was otherwise a landslide victory for the GOP.

Bennett was elected to the State Senate in 1996, and served until he was term limited in 2004.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Presto Search Results |url=https://history.mainelegislature.org/Presto/search/SearchResults.aspx?q=KFNlc3Npb25zLkZpcnN0X05hbWU6KFJpY2hhcmQpKSBBTkQgKFNlc3Npb25zLntMYXN0X05hbWV8Tm90ZXN9OihCZW5uZXR0KSk=&qcf=NjJkY2RjYTMtM2Q5Zi00NmUyLWJjOTctMDA0NWVkZWNmNTRk}}</ref> He became the [[president of the Maine Senate]] in 2001 as the result of a unique power-sharing agreement between Republicans and Democrats predicated on an even split in state senators.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Senate Legislative Record One Hundred and Twentieth Legislature State of Maine |url=https://lldc.mainelegislature.org/Open/LegRec/120/Senate/LegRec_2001-12-06_SP_pS1346-1356.pdf}}</ref> The deal gave the presidency to both parties for one year each during each two-year senate term.<ref>{{cite web |date=November 20, 2002 |title=Senate Republicans pick Bennett, Davis as leaders - Lewiston Sun Journal |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=0GspAAAAIBAJ&sjid=dmoFAAAAIBAJ&pg=2804,3567977&dq=richard-bennett+maine+senate&hl=en |website=news.google.com}}</ref>

On May 2, 2008, he was elected to a four-year term as Maine's Republican national committeeman.{{Citation needed|date=March 2020}}

In [[2006 Maine gubernatorial election|2006]], he considered running for [[Governor of Maine]],<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=fTUpAAAAIBAJ&pg=PA3&dq=Richard+Bennett+Maine+governor&article_id=4173,4633602&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwizmPixxIqOAxVphIkEHZUMKCcQ6AF6BAgLEAM#v=onepage&q=Richard%20Bennett%20Maine%20governor&f=false |title=Mills eyes GOP primary; Bennett pondering run |date=July 30, 2005 |publisher=Lewiston Sun Journal |language=en}}</ref> but decided to remain in the private sector instead, though he did serve as Republican nominee [[Chandler Woodcock]]'s campaign co-chair.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Bennett |first=Richard A. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=PUguAAAAIBAJ&pg=PA4&dq=Richard+Bennett+Maine+governor&article_id=4267,4802792&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjOjvycxYqOAxValokEHQBsJLMQ6AF6BAgGEAM#v=onepage&q=Richard%20Bennett%20Maine%20governor&f=false |title=Poll story misleading |date=May 27, 2006 |publisher=Sun Journal |language=en}}</ref> His name was widely circulated as a possible candidate for the Republican nomination for [[2010 Maine gubernatorial election|governor in 2010]], but he ultimately decided against running.{{Cn|date=June 2025}} In [[2012 United States Senate election in Maine|2012]], Bennett sought the Republican nomination for the [[United States Senate|U.S. Senate]] seat vacated by Olympia Snowe but was defeated in the primary by [[Secretary of State of Maine|Maine Secretary of State]] [[Charlie Summers]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=OurCampaigns |url=https://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=510011 |access-date=2026-03-03 |website=www.ourcampaigns.com}}</ref> Summers ultimately would be defeated by independent [[Angus King]].

On July 20, 2013, Bennett was elected chairman of the [[Maine Republican Party]], replacing former state representative [[Richard Cebra]] of [[Naples, Maine|Naples]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://bangordailynews.com/2013/07/20/politics/rick-bennett-elected-as-new-maine-gop-chairman/|title=Rick Bennett elected as new Maine GOP chairman|date=20 July 2013 |publisher=}}</ref> In 2015, Bennett was unanimously re-elected as chairman of the Maine Republican Party. He was a Republican [[United States presidential elector|elector]] for [[Donald Trump]] in the [[United States presidential election in Maine, 2016|2016 presidential election]] for Maine's second congressional district.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.state.me.us/sos/cec/elec/results/majorpartyelectors16.pdf |title=List of Major Party Presidential Electors |last=Maine Secretary of State}}</ref> He would serve until 2017, being replaced by [[Demi Kouzounas]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2017-01-28 |title=The doctor is in as Maine GOP Chair |url=https://www.newscentermaine.com/article/news/politics/the-doctor-is-in-as-maine-gop-chair/97-394112072 |access-date=2025-11-25 |website=newscentermaine.com |language=en-US}}</ref>

In [[2020 Maine Senate election|2020]], Bennett returned to electoral office for the first time since 2004 by winning a state senate seat, outrunning Donald Trump at the top of the ticket. He was re-elected in [[2022 Maine Senate election|2022]] and [[2024 Maine State Senate election|2024]], significantly outrunning [[2022 Maine gubernatorial election|2022 gubernatorial candidate]] [[Paul LePage]] and Trump in both elections.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Emerson |first=Anthony |date=2025-06-24 |title=What Rick Bennett's independent campaign for Maine Governor means |url=https://anthonyemerson.substack.com/p/what-rick-bennetts-independent-campaign |access-date=2025-06-26 |website=Anthony’s Substack}}</ref>

=== 2026 gubernatorial campaign ===
{{main|2026 Maine gubernatorial election}}
On June 24, 2025, Bennett announced that he would run for [[governor of Maine]] in [[2026 Maine gubernatorial election|2026]] as an [[Independent politician|independent]], ending his lifelong affiliation with the Republican Party.<ref>{{cite news |title=State Sen. Rick Bennett launches gubernatorial campaign for 2026 |url=https://www.newscentermaine.com/article/news/politics/maine-politics/republican-state-senator-rick-bennet-run-for-maine-governor-2026-independent-candidate/97-575d0eb1-e965-417c-a5b1-217d0ad441f3 |access-date=June 24, 2025 |work=[[WCSH]]|date=June 24, 2025}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=2025-06-24 |title=Maine Sen. Rick Bennett announces run for governor as an independent |url=https://www.pressherald.com/2025/06/24/maine-sen-rick-bennett-announces-run-for-governor-as-an-independent/ |access-date=2025-06-25 |website=Press Herald}}</ref> In the months preceding the launch of his campaign, Bennett had publicly diverged from the GOP, crossing party lines to vote against a Republican bill that would have restricted [[Transgender people in sports|transgender students from participating in school sports]], and joining Democrats in voting for a bill that would limit local police in Maine from collaborating with [[Immigrations and Customs Enforcement]].<ref name=":0" /> He was the sole Republican to vote with the Democrats on both bills, on what were otherwise party-line votes.<ref name=":0" />

If elected, Bennett would be Maine's third independent governor, after [[James B. Longley]] (1975–1979) and [[Angus King]] (1995–2003). He would be the first that is a former Republican; both Longley and King were previously Democrats.

In November 2025, Bennett's campaign received the endorsement of the [[Forward Party (United States)|Forward Party]], a "post-partisan" political movement founded by entrepreneur and former Democratic presidential candidate [[Andrew Yang]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mundry |first=Jackie |date=2025-11-21 |title=Former New Jersey governor campaigns in Maine for Rick Bennett |url=https://www.wmtw.com/article/former-new-jersey-governor-campaigns-rick-bennett/69501281 |access-date=2025-11-24 |website=WMTW |language=en}}</ref>

On June 16, 2026, Bennett said he chose to vote in the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] primary rather than the Republican primary.<ref>{{Cite tweet|number=2066944764511965432|user=PHirschkorn|title=1/At a Portland news conference today, Maine Independent candidate for governor Rick Bennett disclosed he voted in the *Democratic* primary for governor but declined to say who he ranked 1st-5th. Bennett said: 2/"Maine has a long tradition of the governor of whatever party and the congressional delegation of whatever party working closely together and setting the partisanship aside when Maine's interests are at stake. I will be that kind of governor." Also praised semi-open primaries.|date=June 16, 2026}}</ref> Both primaries had been held a week before, and voters not enrolled in a party are allowed to choose which party's primary to vote in.

Bennett will face Democratic nominee [[Hannah Pingree]] and Republican nominee [[Robert B. Charles]] in the general election.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rogers |first=Brad |date=2026-06-19 |title=Maine gubernatorial candidates trade barbs on first day of general campaign |url=https://wgme.com/news/local/maine-gubernatorial-candidates-trade-barbs-on-first-day-of-general-campaign-democrat-hannah-pingree-republican-bobby-charles-independent-rick-bennett |access-date=2026-06-20 |website=WGME |language=en}}</ref>

== Private sector ==
Bennett is the president and CEO of ValueEdge Advisors, a firm he founded in summer 2014 to help institutional investors engage with their portfolio companies. From 2006 to 2014 he was CEO of The Corporate Library and then chairman or vice chairman of GMI Ratings, its successor company, an independent research firm focusing on corporate governance, director/executive compensation, and forensic accounting. For six years, Bennett was included in the NACD Directorship's "100 most influential people" in the boardroom and corporate governance community.<ref>{{cite web |title=Directorship Magazine |url=http://www.directorship.com/the-2012-nacd-directorship-100/ |website=www.directorship.com}}</ref>

